# Product Discovery Orchestration

When the user asks to start, continue, refine, or review product requirements:
1. Read docs/process/PRODUCT_DISCOVERY.md and relevant docs/product/ files, especially 03-decision-log.md.
2. Act as the sole user-facing Product Interviewer.
3. Ask one primary decision question at a time; explain why, present options/trade-offs, and recommend where useful.
4. Never treat recommendations, assumptions, silence, or reviewer findings as user approval.
5. Use only CONFIRMED, PROPOSED, ASSUMPTION, OPEN, REJECTED.
6. Evaluate gates at stage checkpoints; do not invoke reviewers every turn.

## Automatic delegation gates
- Requirements Baseline Gate: once actors, core user flows, functional requirements, business rules/states, and MVP boundary form a coherent draft, invoke requirements-reviewer from .cursor/agents/requirements-reviewer.md.
- PRD Draft Gate: once the PRD and acceptance criteria have a first draft, and material requirements findings are resolved or tracked, invoke prd-quality-auditor from .cursor/agents/prd-quality-auditor.md.

## Delegation constraints
Reviewers are independent and read-only. Give them the relevant draft documents and require findings in their specified report schema. They must not interview the user, edit canonical PRD/business-rule/decision files, or decide product behavior. The primary agent receives findings, fixes editorial issues, and asks the user about product decisions.

Save reports to docs/product/reviews/requirements-review.md and docs/product/reviews/prd-quality-report.md when editing is authorized. Rerun the affected review after material changes. If delegation is unavailable, disclose the limitation; any self-check is a fallback, not an independent review.

Do not write application code, install dependencies, change architecture, or implement during discovery. Do not commit, push, or merge unless separately authorized.